{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdHL68hJkzVKdt7SmQZx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator so each heart-rate corridor goes to Intervals as an actual range. Nothing has been pushed, so I haven't seen it on the watch yet. The reasoning and change are written up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Cause:** `gen.py` sent each corridor as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": 68, \"end\": 75}`. In Intervals.icu a target is either a single point (`value`) or a range (`start` + `end`), and mixing `value` with `end` isn't a valid range. The API stored the body as sent, so the readback looked fine. But Intervals treated each step as a single heart-rate point, and the watch shows a single point as the whole zone containing it. That explains why every step came out as one of the watch's zone bands, and why changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands without ever giving 151–160. I'm working from how I understand Intervals' format here, not from docs or a test against the API.\n\n**Fix (`hr_target` in `gen.py`):**\n- Corridors are now sent as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n- If low equals high, it sends a single point; if low is above high, it stops with an error.\n- Targets are still % of LTHR, so Intervals converts them with its LTHR of 172, not the watch's 168. That should give the planned 151–160 for Tempo, 129–141 for Float, and so on.\n\n`python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now writes `start`/`end` on every step.\n\n**Next step:** run `./push.sh` with a new event, sync the watch, and check that the Tempo step shows 151–160.\n\n**Security:** `README.md` and `config/intervals.toml` both contain what look like real Intervals API keys, and both files are in git. I'd rotate those keys, remove them from the repo and its history, and load them from `.env` instead, which git already ignores. I left those files alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_